# Per-user runtime settings. tiko rewrites these as you use it, so tracking them means
# every run shows up as a diff and every pull risks a conflict over someone else's window
# positions and key bindings.
#
# Paths are anchored with a leading "/" on purpose: settings/keywords/*.ini ARE tracked
# (codetips.ini and friends), so a bare *.ini rule would wrongly catch those too.
/settings/keybindings.ini
/settings/settings.ini
# The untitled workspace: whatever loose files the user last had open. Rewritten on every
# exit, so it is runtime state exactly like the two above. (settings/default.session, which
# it replaces, was tracked -- which is why a pull could hand you someone else's open files.)
/settings/default.tiko

# Internal design / planning notes. NEVER published: this repo is public on GitHub
# and clients can browse it.
REFACTOR_PLAN*.md

# Env-gated theme diagnostics (TIKO_THEME_TRACE=1), and the self-test's scratch file.
/theme_dump.txt
/theme_trace.log
/_theme_selftest.tmp

# The Help Center's WebView2 profile: cache, cookies and local storage, created by the
# runtime on first open. Machine-specific and regenerated on demand -- never committed.
# (WebView2Loader.dll IS committed; it is a shipped binary, like bin\Scintilla64.dll.)
/settings/webview2/

# The generated Help Center site: ~31,700 files / ~147 MB, and every helpgen regeneration
# rewrites all of them (the page shell carries cache-busting ?v= hashes), so tracking it
# would add ~31k blobs to history per regen -- permanently, since removing them later means
# a filter-repo force-push that breaks every clone. Staged from C:\dev\HelpCenter\site by
# _copy_helpcenter.bat instead. WITHOUT IT THE HELP CENTER REPORTS ITS FILES AS MISSING,
# so a fresh clone needs that script run once.
/settings/help/helpcenter/

# That claim was FALSE for the whole life of the rule above: an ignore entry does nothing
# to files that are ALREADY TRACKED, and settings\helpcenter had been committed before it
# was written -- ~31,770 blobs, which is why moving the tree surfaced as a mass deletion
# rather than as nothing at all. Same trap as REFACTOR_PLAN.md. It only bites once, but
# check with `git ls-files --error-unmatch <path>` before trusting a rule like this one.

# Bytecode beside settings\help\tikohelp\tools\build.py. The generator's SOURCE is tracked
# -- it is what regenerates that help site -- but its .pyc are per-interpreter and would
# churn on every Python upgrade.
__pycache__/

# Point-in-time engineering audits (markdown + the PDF rendered from it). Kept on disk as
# a record of what a review said on the day, but they are a SNAPSHOT of a tree that has
# moved on since -- tracking them would invite them being read as current, and the status
# pages under C:\dev\docs\status are what say where things actually stand.
/audit/

# Local staging helpers and point-in-time notes, untracked on request. The .bat files copy
# built DLLs / the generated Help Center in from their own repos under C:\dev, so they are
# only meaningful on a machine that has those trees; THEMES.md and CODE_REVIEW.md are
# snapshots of a tree that has moved on. Kept on disk, out of the public repo.
#
# NOTE: these rules do nothing on their own -- all six were ALREADY TRACKED, so they had to
# be `git rm --cached`d first. Same trap as the helpcenter rule above.
/_copy*.*
/THEMES.md
/CODE_REVIEW.md
